 Independent attempts require balancing repeated preparation with shared execution, whose resource growth, contention and cancellation depend on candidate isolation.
 Native execution history includes scope, heuristics, runtime objects and resource counters beyond the logical context, so this tradeoff requires a separate argument about observable verification behavior.
 Established result caches, persistent environments, proof-state snapshots and bounded warm services recover substantial preparation reuse, while the value of independent native execution at equal resources remains unresolved.
-We argue that reusable verification state should retain preparation while separating each candidate's execution history and lifetime from preceding attempts.
